That's why Gwenda was sent to New Zealand.

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:24.760
But Aunt Alison did remember one thing.

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:27.030
They had a house in Devonshire.

00:24:27.030 --> 00:24:30.000
Dartmouth she thought it was, but it could have been Dilmeth.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:32.240
So that all fits.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:36.160
And did Gwenda explain to Aunt Alison why she was making all these inquiries?

00:24:36.160 --> 00:24:39.700
She said something about having a strong feeling she'd been in England before,

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:41.970
but the line wasn't too hot.

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:46.650
Aunt Alison seems to have left one or two things unsaid in the past to Gwenda.

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:51.400
Obviously it wasn't a happy marriage. I suppose she thought it best forgotten.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:53.120
There you are, my dear.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:55.090
Good morning, Miss Marple.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:57.870
Raymond's battering his typewriter, but where's Joan?

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:58.870
In the studio.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:07.100
Since Giles told you all the news and how brilliant you are, we just couldn't get to sleep.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.210
It seemed to explain so much and then so much it didn't.

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:11.890
Ah yes, that's true now.

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:14.230
We've got to find out, of course.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:15.220
Find out what?

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:16.280
The truth.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:17.960
Who was Helen?

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:21.050
And was she really murdered?

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:22.050
I've got to know.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:25.190
Oh my dear, I do advise you.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:28.250
I do advise you most strongly not to do that.

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:29.050
Why?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.650
I expect it's all imagination, but Gwenda won't be happy until she knows.

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:35.540
Charles is right, Miss Marple.

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:38.710
I've got to see it through. Don't you understand?

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:43.830
Oh, yes, of course, I understand, because you're both so young and charming and you're just beginning.

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:47.100
And you simply can't imagine anything.

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:50.200
Yes, I was thinking of Freddy Lovelace.

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:57.560
He was Daryman at Home Farm, and his mother had apparently once won first prize for chrysanthemums.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:25:59.920
Oh, long before Freddy was born.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:01.480
Quite a triumph for her.

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:05.520
And when she died, he tried to find the certificate to have it framed.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:08.340
She was so proud of her, you see.

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:10.120
It was a grave mistake.

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:11.460
Why?

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.170
It turned out she hadn't won?

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:15.780
Oh no, my dear, it turned out she wasn't his mother.

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:20.260
All I'm saying is that I wish,

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:22.410
oh, I do so wish,

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.320
that you'd leave the past alone.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:26.210
Was it here?

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:28.320
A bit nearer the stairs.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.640
- I think it must be true.

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:39.550
What Miss Marple said I mean.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.550
- And you can't remember anything else here in the hall?

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:45.510
- Just Helen.

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:47.410
- Well, no one in Dylmouth seems to know anything,

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:49.660
so it can't have been a murder.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:54.570
The parish registry of deaths wasn't much hope.

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.910
- What idiots.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:59.250
You know who we should ask?

00:26:59.250 --> 00:26:59.800
- Who?

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.000
The land agents.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:05.060
My father must have bought this house, or at least rented it.

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:07.140
Of course.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:10.030
Galbraith and Penderly.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:13.100
It was a holiday.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:15.060
My prep school.

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:18.620
Yorkshire.

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:22.060
Seventy years ago.

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:24.950
God, why come to me?

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:26.990
I'm an old man.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.830
Retired years ago.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:34.770
We thought perhaps you might remember Mr. Galbraith, because my father came here from India.

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:38.410
And Mr. Penderly said you were out there in the First World War.

00:27:38.970 --> 00:27:39.990
India, do you say?

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:44.580
We think Gwenda's father might have rented Hillside.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.750
Well, what are you called, Hillside, then?

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:51.400
Was it, chap?

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:54.070
Young wife and a baby.

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:56.150
Little girl.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:57.140
That was me.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:00.000
You don't say so.

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:02.690
Oh, well.

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:08.970
What's his name?

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.530
Halliday.

00:28:10.530 --> 00:28:11.530
That's right, my dear.

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:12.530
Nice fellow.

00:28:12.530 --> 00:28:15.110
Very pretty wife.

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:16.120
Young.

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:17.120
Fair-haired.

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:21.470
Wanted to be near her people or something like that.

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:22.470
Yes.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:23.470
Very pretty.

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:25.540
Who were her people?

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.560
Hmm?

00:28:27.560 --> 00:28:28.560
No idea.

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:30.000
No idea.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:39.610
at all, Madeleine. Didn't look like you. No, well... St. Catherine's. Yes, that's what

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:47.280
the place was called. It took St. Catherine's at eight guineas a week. While Mrs. Finderson

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:54.030
was out in Egypt. Didn't she run off with someone, that young girl, Halliday's wife?

00:28:54.030 --> 00:29:00.000
We thought you might tell us. Oh, I don't know, my dear.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.780
is a devil of a long time ago anyway.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:26.500
You can tell Beryl I want my drink.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:27.490
Dream it then.

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:35.010
So, the stepmother was young, and she had fear here.

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:39.420
We still don't know her name.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:43.900
We didn't. This is from Raymond.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:47.330
I asked him to make a check for me at Somerset House.

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:48.870
What's Somerset House?

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:51.170
The Registry of Births, Deaths and Marriages.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:55.390
Yes, here it is. A duplicate of your father's certificate.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:29:59.150
Kensington Registry Office, August 7th.

00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:07.840
Kelvin James Halliday to Helen Spenlove Kennedy.

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:11.390
But it can't have been her you saw.

00:30:11.390 --> 00:30:13.470
Galbraith said she went away.

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.510
How do we know?

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:20.230
How do we know she went away?

00:30:56.750 --> 00:30:58.880
It's not quite a surprise to you, I'm sure.

00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:00.000
What are you doing here?

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:05.040
doctor ordered me to the seaside for a change and you always told me how

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:09.960
attractive illness was and I I have a friend who had a cook who now takes

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:14.350
borders on the front so I came here. well why didn't you come and see us? oh I think

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:20.870
the old person is rather a nuisance to a young married couple. no not you. well it would have made me welcome I'm sure.

00:31:20.870 --> 00:31:24.790
well now how are you both getting on? to tell you the truth I was thinking of you

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:29.890
only a moment ago. you were? yes. I've been studying the local newspaper

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:36.720
all the source of information ah then you've seen our advertisement i'm afraid we haven't

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:42.320
altogether taken your advice well good advice is almost certain to be ignored but that's no reason

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:48.340
for not giving it now i always find it personal so intriguing well anyone with the knowledge of

00:31:48.340 --> 00:32:00.000
helen spenlar heliday may kennedy etc etc so it was him yes yes it was well you must forgive me if i seem concerned but by

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:04.830
life has so few excitement and I know you won't think being positive if I asked

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:09.130
you to let me know how you're getting on no of course not if it wasn't for you I'd

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:13.420
probably been a loony bin no we'll tell you everything and you must come to

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:17.930
afternoon tea and see the house we've put the advertisement in the times as

00:32:17.930 --> 00:32:24.240
well so we're bound to get some answers don't you think yes I think so I do

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:29.410
think so my dear Gould's Hill Woodley Bolton

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:36.210
Dear son, in answer to your advertisement in the times, Helen Spenlove Kennedy is my sister.

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:41.000
I've lost touch with her for many years, and should be glad to have news of her.

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:44.390
Yours faithfully, James Kennedy, M.D.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:46.310
Good Lord.

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:48.680
You don't mean to tell me you're Gwenny?

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:51.700
Yes, I'm Gwenny.

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:55.560
This goes for some ceremony. We must have tea. Please sit down.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:56.200
Thank you.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:00.000
So...

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:02.610
You've bought the house in Dilmouth, have you?

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:05.510
Yes. It's quite a strange coincidence.

00:33:05.510 --> 00:33:07.240
We bought Hillside.

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.040
I believe you told me on the phone.

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:12.640
It was for sale, you see.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:16.030
It's the same house my father had all those years ago.

00:33:16.030 --> 00:33:18.530
Hillside? Oh, but surely...

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:21.440
They changed the name, I think, just before the war.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:22.830
I got the right place.

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:25.230
On the Leehampton Road, as you go down into the town.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:28.330
Yes. It used to be called St. Catherine's.

00:33:28.330 --> 00:33:30.000
Of course.

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:36.890
Ah, Mrs. Rowe, we thought we'd have some tea, some cakes, or some hot buttered toast, or something.

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:42.290
I don't usually go in for tea.

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:47.500
What made you want to come back to St. Catharine's? You can't remember much about it, surely.

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:51.020
No, very little. But somehow it felt like home.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:52.460
It felt like home.

00:33:54.440 --> 00:33:58.860
So, I hoped you could tell me all about it. My father and Helen.

00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:03.970
- Well, everything. - Well, there's not much.

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:07.710
Helen was coming back from India. She met your father on the boat.

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:12.220
He'd lost his wife, fell in love with her, and she felt sorry for him.

00:34:12.220 --> 00:34:17.420
And there you are. It's hard to know how these things happen.

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.480
But they married in London, and then they came down to me.

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:23.460
I was in practice in Dilmuth then.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:29.180
A nice fellow, Kelvin. A bit nervy and run down, but they were very happy together then.

00:34:29.180 --> 00:34:30.000
However...

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:32.950
In less than a year, she'd run away with someone else.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.390
You probably know about that.

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:36.350
Who did she run away with?

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:37.550
She didn't tell me.

00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:39.110
I wasn't in her confidence.

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:44.930
But I'd seen, I couldn't help seeing there was some friction between her and Kelvin.

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.210
She didn't want me to know what was going on.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:49.270
I wouldn't have approved.

00:34:50.810 --> 00:34:52.250
They often had people to stay with them.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:53.690
I always imagined it was one of them.

00:34:54.410 --> 00:34:55.830
There wasn't a divorce then?

00:34:56.070 --> 00:34:56.350
Oh, no.

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:57.910
Helen didn't want a divorce.

00:34:58.440 --> 00:34:59.820
Kelvin told me that.

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:03.880
That's why I imagine, perhaps wrongly, it was a case of some married man.

00:35:04.050 --> 00:35:06.670
Someone whose wife was a Catholic, perhaps.

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:10.440
- And my father? - He didn't want a divorce, either.

00:35:11.630 --> 00:35:14.750
Why did he suddenly decide to send me to New Zealand?

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:17.570
Well, I gather your people out there were pressing him,

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:21.310
- and after the break-up of the marriage... - But why didn't he take me himself?

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:26.290
I don't know. He was in rather poor health at the time.

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:30.000
- But what did he die of? - I'm afraid I don't remember all the details.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.700
As I say, he was in poor health, and he went to a sanatorium somewhere on the east coast,

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:38.460
and he died there about two years later.

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:39.830
Where was it exactly?

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:43.280
I'm sorry. I don't remember.

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:45.650
Can you tell us where he was buried?

00:35:45.650 --> 00:35:49.170
You know, I don't think we should dwell on the past.

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:55.320
It was all such a long time ago, and we rather lost touch when he left Dylmouth.

00:35:55.320 --> 00:35:58.520
I've no idea at all where your father is buried.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:00.000
That's strange.

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:02.400
Well, not really. Helen was the link.

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:06.340
I was always very fond of Helen. Tried to take care of her.

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:10.530
She was my half-sister, you know. Much younger than I was.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:14.370
She got involved early on with the most unsuitable young man.

00:36:14.590 --> 00:36:16.990
And I took care of that.

00:36:17.650 --> 00:36:22.850
And then she elected to go to India to marry Walter Fane, son of Dilmuth's leading solicitor.

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:25.150
And frankly, as dull as dishwater.

00:36:25.650 --> 00:36:30.000
When she reached Bombay and saw him again, it was all off. And then...

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:33.960
Well, she met your father coming home.

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:38.810
You see what I mean? I was always worried about Helen.

00:36:38.810 --> 00:36:43.830
Do you know where she is at all? I'd like to get in touch with her again.

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:48.210
No. No, we don't know at all.

00:36:48.210 --> 00:36:52.140
That's one of the reasons we came to see you.

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:56.970
Oh. But I thought from your advertisement.

00:36:56.970 --> 00:36:59.920
So tell me, why did you advertise?

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:04.180
- We were hoping.

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:06.870
We thought someone who knew her

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:10.050
might be able to tell me something about my father.

00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:14.040
- Well, yeah, well now, I'm sorry.

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:21.660
That's all I know.

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.860
- What did you make of it?

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:26.530
- I think it's hiding something.

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:28.200
I wish we'd never started this.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:46.850
I almost strained the chips onto it.

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:50.490
Will anyone with a knowledge of Ellen Spenlove Alliday,

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:54.440
née Kennedy, communicate with Mrs. Newman?

00:37:54.440 --> 00:37:57.850
Well, that's Mrs. Alliday I was in service with at Dilma.

00:37:57.850 --> 00:38:00.000
Her name was Ellen, right enough.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.540
And she was sister to Dr. Kennedy

00:38:02.540 --> 00:38:04.100
and has cured my chilblings.

00:38:05.020 --> 00:38:06.600
You think there's any money in it?

00:38:06.950 --> 00:38:08.480
Might be a will or something.

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:10.080
It's a powerful long time ago.

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:12.640
You don't think it could be

00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:13.960
police, do you, Jim?

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:15.440
Whatever.

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:18.450
You know what I always thought.

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.510
And I said it to Edie, but she wouldn't have it.

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:23.710
And I'd have said a tiny bit more at the time.

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:25.530
Only I nipped out of the pictures

00:38:25.530 --> 00:38:27.950
that night and I didn't want to draw attention to it.

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.000
Now, it wouldn't likely be

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.000
Not after all this time.

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:35.530
But it doesn't say to your advantage.

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:37.610
Do you think I should, Jim?

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:40.360
Not if I was you, Lily.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:41.950
Or not.

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:46.710
Only leads to trouble, that sort of thing.

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:52.500
When's supper ready?

00:39:16.060 --> 00:39:23.360
Hello? Mr. Reid, it's Dr. Kennedy here.

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:25.440
Hello, Dr. Kennedy.

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:44.380
- Seems odd to be healed again.

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:47.170
Well now, I take it you're still determined

00:39:47.170 --> 00:39:49.510
to find the sanatorium where your father died.

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:51.090
- Yes, yes we are.

00:39:51.090 --> 00:39:52.590
- That should be easy enough for you, I imagine,

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:54.100
after what I've told you.

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:55.520
- Shall we sit down?

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:56.670
- Thank you.

00:39:56.670 --> 00:40:00.000
So, I thought it better if you heard the facts

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:05.640
for me. Don't do you or anybody any good, Gwennie. But there it is.

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:20.540
Your father wasn't physically ill.

00:40:20.540 --> 00:40:26.270
The sanatorium in question was a mental home.

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:30.000
A mental home? You mean he was mad?

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:39.380
a very severe mental breakdown he was suffering from delusional obsessions what kind of delusions

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:52.430
he was under the impression that he'd strangled his wife strangled and had he what no no of course

00:40:52.430 --> 00:41:00.000
he hadn't how do you know my dear girl was never any question of such a thing he'd been in a very

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:05.590
unbalanced state for some time and the shock when Helen left him sent him over

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:12.090
the edge if a man would rather his wife was dead than unfaithful he can persuade

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:17.990
himself of anything even that he killed her so you're quite sure there's no

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:22.960
question that he actually did what he said oh quite sure I'd had two letters

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:28.730
from Helen one when she went away after a week to France and another about six

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:29.640
months later

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:33.010
No, the whole thing was a delusion, pure and simple.

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:36.380
Can you tell us about it?

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:38.070
I tried to.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:42.120
He'd been having dreams.

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:46.770
And the dreams always ended up in the same way, with throttling Helen.

00:41:46.770 --> 00:41:52.220
Well, it all came to a head one evening after I got back from the hospital.

00:41:52.220 --> 00:41:55.570
